  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1011 PM MDT Fri Oct 2 2026\n\nExtremely positively tilted trough extends from eastern Great Lakes \nback across the southern Plains to eastern New Mexico. Closed off\nlow in this trough remains over SE New Mexico. Deeper layered \nmoisture remains well to our east ahead of the trough. Over our \narea shallow moisture remains in place behind the back door front \nof yesterday. Extensive CU and SC formed with this moisture with a\nfew stray light showers. Stratus and fog continue with the \nupslope conditions over the eastern Sacs and some lower clouds \ncontinue in other areas east of the Organ/Franklin Mtns. All these\nclouds should gradually dissipated through Saturday mid-day, \nthough models show continued upslope and stratus/fog possible over\nthe eastern Sacs into Sunday. Otherwise partly/mostly sunny \nSaturday and Sunday, though still cool with temperatures around \n5-8 degrees below normal.\n\nMonday through Thursday...nice Fall weather continues as the trough \nslinks southward and high pressure builds in over the Desert \nSouthwest. Temperatures will gradually warm up with highs back to \nnormal by Wednesday. A Pacific trough does ride over the ridge and \ndown over New Mexico Wednesday afternoon. This trough will have very \nlittle moisture with it, but could kick off a few mountain showers \nWednesday. The trough drops south of the area Thursday.\n\nFriday into the weekend...GFS/ECMWF begin wild divergence in \nsolutions after Thursday. GFS drops the aforementioned trough down \nsouth of the Big Bend, closing it off into a low and lifting it back \nup to west Texas by Sunday with a batch of sub-tropical moisture and \na chance of rain. ECMWF takes that same closed low eastward allowing \ndrier southwest flow to develop over our area.",
